Source: lucidos-icon-themes
Section: gnome
Priority: optional
Maintainer: Javid Medina <javidamedina@gmail.com>
Build-Depends: debhelper (>= 10),
               python3,
               python3-gi,
               gir1.2-glib-2.0
Standards-Version: 4.1.1

Package: lucidos-icon-themes
Architecture: all
Depends: ${misc:Depends}, lucid-everything-icon-theme
Description: LucidOS icon themes
 Lucid and Lucid-Dark, the light and dark icon themes drawn for LucidOS.
 .
 They cover the applications LucidOS ships and knows about -- roughly a hundred
 icons -- and they do not attempt the thousands a desktop has to draw an icon
 for. Lucid inherits lucid-everything-icon-theme for those, which is why that
 package is a dependency rather than a recommendation: without it every
 third-party application falls through to hicolor and the desktop looks like a
 stock Ubuntu with a few custom icons.
 .
 These themes are LucidOS's own artwork and are licensed differently from the
 theme they inherit. See /usr/share/doc/lucidos-icon-themes/copyright.

Package: lucid-everything-icon-theme
Architecture: all
Depends: ${misc:Depends}
Description: broad icon coverage beneath the LucidOS themes (GPL-3.0)
 Lucid-Everything, a renamed copy of WhiteSur-icon-theme by vinceliuice. It
 covers the third-party applications that LucidOS does not draw icons for, and
 sits one link down the fallback chain from Lucid and Lucid-Dark:
 .
     Lucid  ->  Lucid-Everything  ->  hicolor
 .
 It is a separate package because it is under a separate licence. This is
 somebody else's work under the GPL-3.0, and the GPL does not permit adding
 restrictions to what it covers -- so it cannot be shipped inside a package
 whose licence is NonCommercial. Splitting the tree along the licence boundary
 makes that a fact dpkg enforces rather than a paragraph in a file.
 .
 Attribution and the list of changes are in
 /usr/share/icons/Lucid-Everything/AUTHORS; the licence text is beside it in
 COPYING.
